Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Sundials Chew the Noon
- Story & Context: When the moon crossed the sun three minutes earlier than predicted over the Yakima fairgrounds, road manager Ezra Vance accidentally severed the midway's diesel generator with an iron tent stake, leaving the Ferris wheel stranded in an overpowering smell of spilled motor oil and fried dough. Rather than rioting, six carny drummers rolled battered field snares directly into Ezra's guitar tent, locking into a furious 160-BPM stomp while Ezra engaged his fuzz pedal and screamed, "Wind the mainspring till the teeth snap off!" into a megaphone. That frantic, battery-powered jam became the foundational document of the entire scene, transforming equipment failure into a jubilant, mud-flinging riot under the darkened sky.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Dynamic album art illustration of a giant stone sundial cracking open inside an overgrown carnival midway during an eclipse, golden light radiating from the fractures while drumsticks and confetti explode outward into a grainy 1990s print aesthetic.

Track 2: "Who Set the Alarm for Noon?"
- Story & Context: Sitting on a splintered luggage cart at the Sunnyside depot with sticky cherry soda dripping down her bruised knuckles, Mara Finch realized she had intentionally missed the band's only outbound freight ride to Portland just to re-tune her marching floor toms to the reverberation of the town clock. Mara tracked the song live in the depot waiting room, laughing through tears as she shouted the confession, "I traded our return tickets for a bigger snare!" over a galloping wall of accordion squeals and roaring, fuzz-soaked bass chords. The track captures the exact moment the band abandoned standard industry timetables in favor of seasonal celestial transits, celebrating their self-inflicted isolation with explosive horn fanfares.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Artistic photograph of a broken vintage brass alarm clock resting on a weathered train platform, surrounded by crushed red soda cans and splintered drumsticks, bathed in dramatic midday twilight and warm analogue grain.

Track 3: Splice-Mark on the Stolen Calendar
- Story & Context: By the time the final totality faded over the gravel pits of Black-Sun Crater, Deputy Sheriff Henderson had climbed the back of the flatbed trailer to confiscate the band's PA, his uniform drenched in sweat and smelling of hot brake fluid. Instead of shutting down, accordionist Clara shoved a pair of marching cymbals into the deputy's hands, and the band launched into an unhinged, double-time reprise with the entire crowd chanting, "Tear the months away, we own the afternoon!" until even the deputy couldn't stop tapping his boots. The tape machine ran out of reels during the final drum roll, leaving an abrupt magnetic tape hiss at the track's tail that became an accidental legend across Northwest tape-trading circles.
